

# Chapter 2: Basics_02 -> python inner workings

#         1. Python shell is opened by opening terminal in whatever OS you are using and typing 'python'(windows based terminal / cmd)/ 'python3'(linux based terminal) and pressing enter. it is also called python interpreter. it is also called python prompt. it is also called python interactive shell. it is also called python interactive mode. it is also called python command line. it is also called python console. it is also called python repl (read, evaluate, print, loop). it is also called python cli (command line interface).

#         2. python shell is used to run python code line by line. it is terminated by pressing ctrl/cmd + z(normally makes the state 'suspeneded'). it is also terminated by typing 'exit()' and pressing enter. it is also terminated by typing 'quit()' and pressing enter. it is also terminated by pressing ctrl/cmd + c. it is also terminated by pressing ctrl/cmd + d.

#         3. python shell is used to test python code. it is maintained by providing proper indentation. proper indentation is required as python is a whitespace dependent language. it is also called pythonic code. least indented code is first priority then the next least indented code and so on. it is also called 'off-side rule'. it is also called 'significant whitespace'.

#         4. sometimes python does not updates it's previous made binaries. so we need to restart the shell or you can use 'reload()' function to reload the module. it is also called 're-importing'. reload() is a built-in function. it is located under 'importlib' module. it maybe also called as'importlib.reload()' function.